---
/**
 * The rail shell — an <aside> beside the prose that disperses below md, with its
 * contents pinned while the page scrolls.
 *
 * ── EXTRACTED ON ITS SECOND IDENTICAL CONSUMER ───────────────────────────────
 *
 * The article page built this and the presentation page repeated it byte for byte:
 * the same two elements, the same `display: contents` dispersal, the same sticky
 * child at the same offset, the same 48 between groups. That is the project's
 * promotion trigger, and the same one that produced <RailNav> — which is what goes
 * INSIDE this, and is a different thing.
 *
 * ── WHAT IT OWNS: THE BEHAVIOUR. WHAT IT DOES NOT: THE PLACEMENT ─────────────
 *
 * No `grid-column` here, matching TopicList's rule that placement belongs to the
 * caller. Which columns a rail occupies is a fact about the page's grid — and the
 * pages differ: an article auto-places into row 1, while a page with a transparent
 * <PageHeader> needs `grid-row: 2 / -1` to start at the lede. Neither is this
 * component's business.
 *
 * The caller reaches the root because `{...rest}` forwards its scope attribute. A
 * component that does not spread rest silently cannot be styled by its parent; the
 * house pattern, and PageHeader's header records the bug.
 *
 * ── THE TWO INVARIANTS COME WITH IT, AND BOTH FAIL SILENTLY ──────────────────
 *
 * ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
 * │  1. `.rail` MUST REMAIN A STRETCHED GRID ITEM. Do not let a caller add    │
 * │     `align-self: start`.                                                  │
 * │  2. NO `overflow` other than `visible` on any ancestor between the sticky  │
 * │     child and the page.                                                    │
 * └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
 *
 * A sticky element travels only inside its containing block, which is `.rail`'s box.
 * A grid item stretches to its row by default, so the rail is as tall as the prose
 * beside it and the child has the whole article to travel down. With
 * `align-self: start` the box collapses to its own content height and the child
 * scrolls away like anything else, with nothing on screen to say why. Measured at a
 * 3000px prose column when the article page first built this.
 *
 * ── ONE SLOT, AND THE PAGE TEMPLATE IS WHY THERE IS NOT YET A SECOND ─────────
 *
 * Everything slotted here pins together, which is what both current consumers want:
 * an article and a presentation put "On This Page" and Topics in one group.
 *
 * The `page` template's rail is the shape that does NOT fit — its hero sits above
 * the nav and must scroll away while the nav pins, so it needs a second, unpinned
 * slot. That is deliberately not built: a slot with no content to put in it is a
 * guess at what the content would look like, the same rule that keeps the `table`
 * serializer unwritten. Add it when that page adopts this, not before.
 *
 * `reviews.astro` is a third shape again — it pins the <aside> ITSELF and hides it
 * outright below md rather than dispersing. Adopting this there would mean wrapping
 * its nav and moving the stickiness onto the wrapper; behaviourally equivalent, and
 * a change to a page already signed off, so it is offered rather than assumed.
 */
import type {HTMLAttributes} from 'astro/types'

interface Props extends HTMLAttributes<'aside'> {
  class?: string
  /* Forwarded to the root, which is what lets a page place this — see the note above. */
  [key: string]: unknown
}

const {class: className, ...rest}: Props = Astro.props
---

{
  /*
    <aside>, AND IT COSTS NO LANDMARK. An unnamed <aside> maps to `complementary`
    only when its nearest sectioning ancestor is <body>. Both current consumers sit
    inside <article> or <Band as="section">, so it maps to `generic` and the <nav>s
    within stay the landmarks a screen-reader user navigates by.

    That is also why there is deliberately no `aria-label`: naming it would promote
    it to a landmark competing with the two real ones inside it.
  */
}
<aside class:list={['rail', className]} {...rest}>
  {
    /*
      A <div> and not a <section>: it is a positioning wrapper with no thematic
      content of its own, and <section> is explicitly not a generic container — it
      would put a nameless entry in the outline.
    */
  }
  <div class="rail-sticky">
    <slot />
  </div>
</aside>

<style>
  /*
   * ── BELOW md THE RAIL DISPERSES RATHER THAN COLLAPSING ─────────────────────
   *
   * The obvious implementation — let `--col-rail` collapse to `1 / -1` and drop the
   * whole rail under the prose — is the one the design rejects. The mobile boards send
   * the groups SEPARATE WAYS: "On This Page" disappears entirely, and Topics reappears
   * at the foot of the prose as an inline comma-separated run. They are not one block
   * that reflows.
   *
   * `display: contents` erases both boxes while keeping their children, so each group
   * becomes a grid item of the caller's <Grid> in its own right — free to place itself,
   * or to not render. One DOM, no duplicated link list, nothing hidden that a screen
   * reader would still read.
   *
   * Both elements are plain containers with no role, so the historic `display: contents`
   * accessibility bug — the element vanishing from the tree — has nothing to take. The
   * <nav>s inside keep their boxes and their landmarks.
   *
   * MOBILE-FIRST, per tokens.css: an engine that never evaluates the container query has
   * to be left with the phone layout. The cost is that md says `display: block` rather
   * than unsetting anything.
   */
  .rail,
  .rail-sticky {
    display: contents;
  }

  @container style(--breakpoint-md: true) {
    .rail,
    .rail-sticky {
      display: block;
    }

    /*
     * `top` is a judgment the boards cannot show, since they draw no scroll position.
     * The masthead does not stick, so `top: 0` would pin the group flush against the
     * viewport edge; `rhythm-band` gives it the breathing room the bands have. Judged
     * on screen and settled (Andy, 2026-08-26).
     */
    .rail-sticky {
      position: sticky;
      top: var(--rhythm-band);
    }

    /*
     * 48 between rail groups — `heading-minor` because what follows is a heading. Both
     * are 48, and DESIGN.md's rule is to pick by prefix and name rather than by value.
     *
     * `:global()` IS REQUIRED HERE AND IS NOT A SHORTCUT. Slotted content carries the
     * CALLER's scope attribute, never this component's, so a plain `nav + nav` would
     * compile to `nav[cid-rail] + nav[cid-rail]` and match nothing — the rule would
     * ship and be silently dead. Scoping it under `.rail-sticky`, which does carry this
     * component's attribute, keeps the global half contained to inside a rail.
     */
    .rail-sticky :global(nav + nav) {
      margin-top: var(--rhythm-heading-minor);
    }
  }
</style>
